{
  "dom_id": "step-q125935",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226762/assets/2b65eef55ba7db5252b4e472170ca699a7e21c2379648734101fa26bc456fa94.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: In the first quadrant, the curves intersect when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{9}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$*",
  "worked_solution": "We are given that our curves intersect when ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226762/assets/26c9070bb1406543061cf4072f0cab9162d63190f4d6ffc4601e88896a38a635.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}),$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{9},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}))\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
    {
      "key": "selection",
      "type": "radio",
      "choices": [
        {
          "option": "a",
